---
import "../../styles/global.css";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />

    <meta
      name="viewport"
      content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"
    />

    <title>
      Africaya Renovation — Go Izumita / 泉田剛
    </title>

    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Renovation proposal for an existing wooden signboard-style building, combining a three-generation residence, African textile shop, guesthouse, community space, and dance studio."
    />

    <meta
      property="og:title"
      content="Africaya Renovation — Go Izumita / 泉田剛"
    />

    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="Architectural Renovation Proposal"
    />

    <meta
      property="og:image"
      content="https://goizumita.com/images/africa-1.jpg"
    />

    <meta
      property="og:type"
      content="website"
    />

    <meta
      property="og:url"
      content="https://goizumita.com/projects/africaya-renovation"
    />

    <link
      rel="icon"
      href="/favicon.ico"
    />

    <link
      rel="icon"
      type="image/png"
      sizes="48x48"
      href="/favicon.png"
    />
  </head>

  <body>
    <Header />

    <main>
      <h1>
        Africaya Renovation
      </h1>

      <p>
        Renovation proposal for a
        signboard-style wooden house
        renovated and extended in 1956.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project was conceived as a
        three-generation house and a
        space for an African community,
        combining a textile shop,
        residence, guesthouse,
        community space, dance studio,
        and other shared functions
        within a single existing
        building.
      </p>

      <p>
        The proposal reconsiders the
        identity of the existing
        building through three
        architectural elements:
        foundation, facade, and
        furniture.
      </p>

      <p>
        Granary and Stone Foundation.
        The existing timber structure
        is reinforced and lifted using
        traditional ageya
        building-lifting techniques.
        The existing foundations are
        removed, and the building is
        reconstructed using a
        traditional ishibadate stone
        foundation system.
      </p>

      <p>
        The idea refers to African
        granaries and their relationship
        to stones and the ground,
        connecting the construction
        method with the spatial and
        cultural context of Africaya.
      </p>

      <p>
        Textile and Facade.
        The new facade is made from
        perforated metal based on
        textile patterns sold at
        Africaya.
        By translating African textile
        patterns into an architectural
        surface, the proposal
        reinterprets and expands the
        characteristic signage facade
        of the existing building in a
        contemporary way.
      </p>

      <p>
        Furniture and Freedom.
        Despite the limited floor area,
        the building accommodates a
        wide range of programs,
        including a three-generation
        residence, shop, guesthouse,
        community space, and dance
        studio.
      </p>

      <p>
        Rather than dividing the
        interior into fixed rooms,
        the proposal creates an open
        and flexible space defined
        primarily by movable furniture.
        Rearranging these elements
        allows the same interior to
        adapt to different activities,
        occupants, and relationships.
      </p>

      <p>
        Academic project from the
        Department of Architecture at
        Musashino Art University, 2022.
        Assignment: Akira Suzuki Seminar.
        Supervision: Gross Studio.
      </p>

